ignals that a he was very  unhappy America was supporting India and  B he therefore wanted to be compensated  as we like to say for Pakistan's  neutrality and compensation meant give  me Kashmir Kennedy refused to give in to  this blackmail and he sent repeated  messages to the Pakistanis that if you  enter this war we will regard you as  part of the enemy not as part of the  alliance that we have with you the 20th  of November was sheer panic in New Delhi  John Kenneth Galbraith writes in his  memoirs that it looked like India was  going to disintegrate all Indian  aircrafts private and public were  commissioned commissioned by the Indian  Air Force martial law was declared in  much of northeast India and India  belatedly began moving troops from the  Pakistan front even though it feared a  second front could open up and then that  night for no explanation  China unilaterally stopped the Chinese  ceased their offensive and announced  that within a month they would draw back  to the lines where they had started we  don't know why China did that Chinese  records are not available today you can  get a lot of records about what the  American government thought about what  the Indian government thought but  Chinese archives remain sealed Nehru  credited John F Kennedy with convincing  the Chinese to stop he told John Kenneth  Galbreath that it was America's resolve  and Kennedy's determination not to let  the Chinese win manifested in the  airlift and manifested in the deployment  of the aircraft carrier battle group  that persuaded Beijing to stop and not  let this crisis continue to go any  further this of course leaves us with  the great what if what if the Chinese  hadn't stopped that night would the  United States have found itself at war  with Communist China  remember in 1962 only been nine years  before when we had been at war with  Communist China in Korea fought a war in  Korea with the Communist Chinese for the  better part of three years and here  Nehru was essentially asking us to go to  war with China again we'll never know  it's a what-if but in my judgment  Kennedy almost certainly would have said  yes and I make that judgment for several  reasons first because he really did  believe that Indian democracy was  crucial to the United States in the  global balance of power second because  his ambassador John Kenneth Galbraith  who was a personal friend and one of the  people closest to the president would  almost certainly have recommended it and  third because just a year later in the  fall of 1963 the United States Air Force  the Royal Air Force and small squadrons  from the Royal Australian Air Force and  the Royal Canadian Air Force deployed  into India for a military training  exercise which was exactly what Nehru  had asked for a year before so we  actually practiced going into this war  the conflict between China and India  ended in a ceasefire unilaterally on the  21st of November 1962 but the conflicts  not over China and India have never  settled their border dispute this border  dispute remains the longest unsettled  border dispute in the world today  neither party agrees to wear that border  should be there have been hundreds of  meetings between the Chinese and Indians  and they haven't succeeded one iota  moving forward they are big trading  partners with each other today the  likelihood of another China India war is  probably pretty low I wouldn't say the  same thing about the likelihood of  another india-pakistan war that I think  is probably a very serious reality so  that dispute continues to this day and  in addition that dispute has led to two  other things and access between China  and Pakistan China and Pakistan had  barely any interaction with each other  before 1962 but if you look back in 50  years perspective it's 1962 or the  alliance between China and Pakistan  begins and today that Alliance is very  strong they call it the all-weather  alliance it's kind of a snub that the  United States we're not all whether they  are all whether they say it's taller  than the Himalayas and deeper than the  Indian Ocean and just this year China  and Pakistan signed agreements which  will lead to 46 billion dollars in  Chinese investment in Pakistani  infrastructure and of course it heads  another dimension their secret nuclear  collusion Pakistan and China have been  secret nuclear partners since the 1960's  China gave Pakistan the designs for its  nuclear weapons force and today Pakistan  has the fastest growing nuclear weapons  program in the world faster than any  other country this is also led to a  triangular arms race probably the most  dangerous arms race in the world today  between China and Pakistan on the one  hand and India on the other just two  years ago India proudly announced that  had fired an intermediate-range  ballistic missile that allowed it for  the first time to target Beijing with  nuclear weapons so the crisis that was  averted in 1962 remains a problem even  to this day  john f kennedy's forgotten store crisis  is the story of that crisis mostly from  the American point of view it also has  several other backstories which I'd be  happy to talk about in Q&amp;A like the role  played by the first lady who's very  important in this whole issue and the  role played by the CIA in trying to  sponsor her rebellion in Tibet but let  me just finish with one final word about  sources this book relies upon letters  that have only been Declassified in the  last couple of years between Kennedy and  Nehru for more than 50 years the Indian  government denied these letters even  existed because in some ways they're  very very embarrassing India at its  moment of greatest need  had to ask United States to bail it out  the letters are declassified and this is  the first book that takes advantage of  looking at those documents in depth with  that thank you very much for your  attention and I'd be delighted to take  questions if I can come up with answers  [Applause]  and if not only started I would love to  hear about the first lady you know right  John F Kennedy never went to India and  Pakistan during the thousand days that  he was in office he he had gone to India  in the early 1950s and mrs. Kennedy  actually recounts what happened she  didn't go with him but it's a great  anecdote she said that when the then  Senator John F Kennedy got to New Delhi  and they requested a meeting with the  Prime Minister  the u.s. embassy told him that the prime  minister's attention often failed pretty  quickly and that if the prime minister's  attention was failing he'd start to look  up at the ceiling and start playing with  his pencil within two minutes of the  meeting with John F Kennedy  according to Jackie's Nehru was staring  up at the ceiling and playing with his  pencils so it was not a very auspicious  part for their relationship it got  better but mrs. Kennedy's did travel to  South Asia during the thousand days of  the kennedy administration she went in  the spring of 1962 so just a few months  before the crisis it was the first time  a first lady had traveled by herself  outside the United States in the age of  television Eleanor Roosevelt had  traveled during the Second World War but  there was no television and Truman and  Eisenhower zwipes never traveled by  themselves overseas now when I say by  herself that's a little bit of a  misnomer her sister went whither a  Secret Service detachment of about 25  people they had they needed a Boeing 707  to get them there as well  her trip was a spectacular success  she was hailed as the queen of America  in India and Pakistan and she excited  the crowds not just in India and  Pakistan but because of television she  excited the people back in the United  States of America  in Pakistan I obtained also gave her her  gift he'd heard that she was a horse  woman that she loved horses that she  loved riding that she loved jumping with  them so he gave her a ten year old  gelding named xodar the DAR means chief  in dari and she laid immediate  instructions back to the White House  that sadar was to be given VIP treatment  flown home on its errand airplane and  there was going to be nothing about the  silly rules about checking the  veterinary background - this horse was  to be shipped immediately to her farm  well it is the DAR that bride's  riderless behind john f kennedy's  caisson during the funeral of  Pennsylvania Avenue and over to  Arlington Park it's kind of a unique  reminder of the role India and Pakistan  played in the in the kennedy  administration the other thing she did  of course was she was a hostess she  hosted both Ayub Khan and Nehru for  state visits to the United States the  state visit for field marshal Ayub Khan  was unique only once in American history  has Mount Vernon George Washington's  home been used for state visit  Jackie wanted to use Mount Vernon she  had just come back from a trip with her  husband to Versailles in France and the  Schonbrunn palace in Austria but we  don't have any palaces but she wanted to  do something equally grand so in June  1961 Mount Vernon was the site of a very  special summit between Ayub Khan and  John F Kennedy a few months later Nehru  came it was in November and Nehru they  had to now of course if you'd had  Mountain Vernon for the Pakistani what  do you do to outdo that  so they took Nehru to Jackie's home in  Newport  Rhode Island Hammersmith farms it's  called a cottage but if you've ever  driven around Newport you'll realize  their idea of a cottage is a lot  different than most of us but it turned  out to be a complete failure nehru at  this point was getting on in age he was  very tired he was very exhausted from  the flight he answered most of Kennedy's  requests and most of Kennedy's thoughts  with mono syllabic short answers there  was no engagement and then it got worse  after the stop in Newport they came down  to the White House to have official  dinner in the White House as well and  somebody on the White House staff failed  to properly open the flue in the  fireplace in the East Room and the  meeting began with smoke spreading  throughout the White House was not a  very auspicious starting to their  relationship but mrs. Kennedy in this  regard was was it was a crucial partner  with her husband in the American  engagement with both India and Pakistan  in the early 1960s  yeah I wonder if these documents are  wondering if these documents shed any  light on the CIA involvement in Tibet  and on the GA support for the khumba's  in their guerrilla fight against the  Chinese sure let me back up just a  little bit in the 1950s Pakistan was  America who is called America's most  allied ally we actually had more  treaties of alliance with Pakistan than  we did with any other country in the  world Pakistan was a member of the  central treaty organization was a member  of the Southeast Asia Treaty  Organization we were firmly in bed with  the Pakistanis the Eisenhower  administration liked Pakistan because it  was clearly with us in the Cold War  whereas India was a much more neutral  posture the CIA as a result developed a  very close relationship with its  Pakistani counterpart in the 1950s one  of these relationships was the famous  YouTube a youtube aircraft were based in  Peshawar in Pakistan that's where they  took off to fly over the Soviet Union  that's where the the famous mission who  was shot down began in 1959 there was a  second secret operation and that was to  support the Tibetan resistance 1950  China moved troops into Tibet the story  of whether Tibet is part of China or not  part of China you can go to the to a  library there are bookshelves full of  books arguing both sides of it I'm not  going to go into that today from the  Tibetan standpoint it was pretty  clear-cut their country was invaded by  the Chinese their way of life was  attacked by China in many ways and their  spiritual leader the Dalai Lama was  forced into exile or went into exile  what few people know is that when the  Dalai Lama went into exile it was a CIA  team of trained Tibetans that  helped him get out of Tibet and cross  over the border into India where he's  remains to this day the CIA was also  providing military support to Tibetan  rebels fighting against the Communist  army the People's Liberation Army and  that support was provided from an  airbase in Pakistan in what is today  Bangladesh what at the time was East  Pakistan if you think about the  geography where can you get at to that  easily from Bangladesh or East Pakistan  is placed very close by the CIA trained  some of the operatives in the United  States some of them were trained at the  CIA's training facility in Camp Perry in  Virginia but most of them were trained  in Colorado and the logic of that was  that Colorado is as close to Tibet in  terms of geography and climate as you  can find anywhere in the United States I  don't know whether that's really true or  not but that was the logic they were  trained at at a US Army facility that  had been used to keep captured German  soldiers in World War two they were  trained there they were then flown to  East Pakistan and then parachuted into  Tibet in many ways it was a crazy  operation there was no possible way the  Tibetans with all the American support  in the world we're going to actually  defeat the Chinese People's Liberation  Army John Kenneth Galbraith thought the  whole idea was was crazy even called it  unsanitary not sure that why exactly he  picked that but it's one of his  arguments the operation continued after  the Eisenhower administration into the  Kennedy administration the documents  that I alluded to about relationship  between arrow and Kennedy don't speak to  it very much  officially the Indians were out of the  loop I'm increasingly convinced that the  Indians must have been aware of at least  part of what was going on after all  they're immediately next door but  they were they were not involved in the  operation at this point but we do have  fortunately a - very very good accounts  of the operation written by CIA officers  who were involved in it and who got  their memoirs published cleared by the  CIA's review process which provide a  very very in-depth report on what was  going on just to go back take you back  to Mountain Vernon for one more minute  the business between Kennedy and Ayub  Khan that night was top secret I  obtained the month before the mountain  Fernand Sunnat had turned off use of the  Air Force Base in East Pakistan he  wanted to send Kennedy a message if you  want to flirt with the Indians if you  want to give India economic assistance  it's going to cost you something he  didn't want to do this in public he  didn't want to have a public falling-out  so the easiest way to send the message  was to cut off a covert program the  Americans would know the rest of the  world wouldn't necessarily know it  wouldn't become a big issue Kennedy at  that dinner that night in addition to a  lot of Senators and cabinet members also  invited the Director of Central  Intelligence Allen Dulles and when  everyone else was having their mint  juleps and orange juice before dinner  Kennedy took Ayub Khan for a walk in the  gardens around Mount Vernon and asked  him would you let us continue the  operation to fight Communist Chinese  would you resume use of the Tibet pace  and either because of the charm of the  night or the charm of the President or  maybe because he always intended to he  agreed to and resumed the assistance  resumed that operation now flash forward  to April Harriman what Averell Harriman  arrives in India he has three items on  his agenda one is a political agenda how  to work between India and Pakistan  second is a military general how much  more military assistance to get to the  Indians what kind of assistance  what kind of joint training operations  should we pursue and the covert agenda  because now that China and India had  gone to war the CIA and the White House  judge the Indians were ready to replace  the Pakistanis and they were right and  the Indians now began to support the  Tibetan resistance and CIA flights now  started coming out of India to drop  equipment to the Chinese the other thing  that began was u2 flights heretofore  YouTube flights had only come out of  Pakistan in 1963 in 1964  YouTube YouTube flights now had come out  secretly from air bases in India and it  was one of those air flights from a  Indian facility that discovered that the  Chinese were in the final stages of  preparing to test their first nuclear  weapon and gave the United States the  crucial intelligence that China was on  the verge of becoming a nuclear weapons  state  idea  the only other thing I'd like to share  just say a word about the ambassador  in New Delhi John Kenneth Galbraith  because he's a huge part of this book  John Kenneth Galbraith was a tall man  six foot six he was born in Ontario in  Canada he's a graduate of the Ontario  School of Agriculture he then moved to  the United States became an economist  wrote several books in the 1940s and  1950s about economics and became one of  the stars of Harvard University one of  if not the star of Harvard University in  the 1950s and he also became an advisor  to Kennedy he wrote he kept a diary  while he was ambassador to New Delhi and  he later published that diary for a  historian of course the diary is gold  because it's not a memoir a memoirs  written later and you put in how  brilliant you were whether or not you  were or not a diary you write at the  time and you may still say you were  brilliant but at least we know you  actually said that on the 19th of  November 1962 or otherwise the diary is  a fascinating piece of very unpolitical  and politically unaware politically  unknown dialogue he writes his  impressions of everybody and everything  the State Department for example he  constantly criticized he says working  with the State Department is like  fornicating through a mattress he also  talks about people and he has a very  1960s attitude towards gender for  example he describes flying from  Washington to Hawaii with a American  movie actress at the time and in the  diary says I fell in love before we even  took off and the magic of this voyage  you have to wonder what did his wife  think when she read his diary years  later but it also provides an enormous  amount of in  Nation about what the Indians were  thinking what they were up to how the  United States government worked how the  Kennedy administration worked the inside  workings of the kennedy administration  by somebody who wasn't sitting in the  White House but had a very different  optic sitting in New Delhi watching what  was going on in the White House and one  of the things that really appalled John  Kenneth Galbraith was he could see the  Kennedy administration's sliding into  the Vietnam War and he kept writing the  president saying don't do it don't do it  don't listen to the generals all the  generals are going to get you into a  mess and it's an interesting question of  course another one of the great what-ifs  of the Kennedy administration if Kennedy  had lived would the United States have  gone into the Vietnam War the way that  it tragically did under the Johnson  administration so that's another  perspective that I think you'll find in  this book that you don't find anywhere  else thank you thank you very much for  coming thank you thank you again so much  for coming if you'd like to get a book  please come up say hello get one side if  you can do this a favor and help us by  folding up the chairs and place them  against the bookcases that would be  great have more room to sign thank you  let's have another round of applause  [Applause]  Oh  [Music]
 

